Type Meaning Output <code>1</code> Parent <code>pk</code> matches one related <code>fk</code> Object or <code>null</code> <code>11</code> Same as <code>1</code>, looked up per parent with <code>findOne</code> Object or <code>null</code> <code>1n</code> Many related records share the parent <code>pk</code> Array <code>nm</code> Many-to-many through a pivot collection Array <code>n1</code> Parent <code>pk</code> is an array of ids, first match wins Object or <code>null</code> <pre><code>// Order belongs to one customer: orders.customerId -&gt; users._id
customer: {
    path: ["main", "users"],
    type: "1",
    pk: "customerId",
    fk: "_id"
}

// Order has many items: items.orderId -&gt; orders._id
items: {
    path: ["main", "items"],
    type: "1n",
    pk: "_id",
    fk: "orderId"
}
</code></pre>

Prefix Property Used by <code>s.</code> <code>search</code> find, findOne, update, updateOne, updateOneOrAdd, remove, removeOne, toggleOne, count, relation. Required for <code>updateOneOrAdd</code> and <code>toggleOne</code> <code>d.</code> <code>data</code> add, toggleOne <code>u.</code> <code>updater</code> update, updateOne, updateOneOrAdd <code>o.</code> <code>options</code> find, relation <code>r.</code> <code>relations</code> relation query <code>e.</code> <code>select</code> all operations that return records <p>Dots also create nested objects:</p> <pre><code>db users s.address.city="NYC"
</code></pre> <p>This produces:</p> <pre><code>{ db: "db", d: { find: { collection: "users", search: { address: { city: "NYC" } } } } }
</code></pre> <p>Numeric path segments create arrays, which is useful for logical operators:</p> <pre><code>db users s.$and.0.status="active" s.$and.1.role="admin"
</code></pre> <p>Comparison operators are rewritten to the operator-first form, so do not mix them with a nested <code>$and</code> path. Use the JSON form instead:</p> <pre><code>db users s={"$and":[{"status":"active"},{"age":{"$gt":18}}]}
</code></pre>

Operator Meaning <code>$set</code> Set fields (same as plain assignment) <code>$unset</code> Remove fields <code>$rename</code> Rename a field <code>$inc</code> Increment a number <code>$dec</code> Decrement a number <code>$push</code> Append to an array <code>$pushSet</code> Append values that are not already present <code>$pushAll</code> Append all values <code>$pull</code> Remove matching values <code>$pullAll</code> Remove all matching values <code>$merge</code> Shallow merge an object <code>$deepMerge</code> Deep merge an object <pre><code>{
    updater: {
        name: "Bob",
        $inc: { loginCount: 1 },
        $set: { profile: { lastLogin: { __: "_now" } } }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Updater keys are not split on dots. Use nested objects for a nested update. In VQLS, <code>u.$set.profile.lastLogin=...</code> works because the parser builds the nesting.</p>
